# Story: someone who never leaves the keyboard docks an editor, a terminal and a browser, walks around
# them with focus, trades places, makes the editor bigger, evens things out, turns the row into a
# column, and sends the editor to a second display.
# Reference: F22 (A220 to A225). Window ids start at 101. `focus 101` is a click; `focus right` is the command.
open Editor
open Terminal
open Browser
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
dock 103 beside 102 bottom
focus 101
# Two windows are equally near on the right: the one that had the keyboard last wins (docking gave it to the browser).
focus right
expect ok
expect focused 103
focus up
expect focused 102
focus left
expect focused 101
focus left
expect fail
expect focused 101
# Trade places with the window on the right that had focus last. The slots keep their sizes.
swap right
expect ok
expect left-of 102 101
expect above 101 103
expect focused 101
swap left
expect left-of 101 102
expect above 102 103
# Bigger, then even again.
grow 100
expect ok
balance
expect ok
# Framed on one window, the same command walks from space to space, as `neighbor` does.
zoom 101
focus right
expect ok
expect hidden 101
expect visible 102
expect visible 103
show-all
# Turn the row into a column and back, then reverse it.
focus 101
rotate
expect ok
expect above 101 102
expect left-of 102 103
rotate
expect left-of 101 102
expect above 102 103
mirror
expect left-of 102 101
# A second display to the right: send the editor there, then go back.
displays 2
move-to-display next
expect ok
expect docked 101
expect focused 101
expect left-of 102 101
# Back on the first display, to the window there that had the keyboard last.
focus-display previous
expect ok
expect focused 102
move-to-display 3
expect fail
